1. AI Tools and Approved Uses
The team may use AI tools to assist with development, 
    documentation, debugging, and code review. AI-generated 
    work must still be reviewed and understood by a team 
    member before being incorporated into the project.

- Approved Tools -

GitHub Copilot
- may be used for
    - Generating code suggestions
    - Explaining existing code
    - Suggesting tests
    - Identifying bugs
    - Reviewing code
- Copilot suggestions must be reviewed by the developer
    before being committed
- The developer remains responsible for the correctness
    of the resulting code

ChatGPT
- may be used for
    - Generating code suggestions
    - Explaining existing code
    - Suggesting tests
    - Identifying bugs
    - Reviewing code
- ChatGPT should not be the final authority on technical 
    designs. A team member must verify that the tech Does
    work and that the proposed solution fits the requirement

GitHub
- may be used for - 
    - Source-code hosting
    - Pull-requests and code review
    - Tracking issues and project work
    - Recording the team's development history
- AI-generated code may be commited to the repository only
    after the responsible team member has reviewed and 
    approved that the code works 

- Prohbited Uses -

AI tools may not be used for
- Submit work that a team member does not understand
- Automatically merge code without the required review process
- Generate or expose passwords, API keys, authentication tokens, 
    or other secrets
- Upload confidential credentials or sensitive project information 
    to an AI service

2. Promt-Log Policy
The team will maintain a record of significant AI use so that the 
    development process remains transparent and reproducible

- What Gets Logged -
For AI-assisted development, the team will record:
- The date of the interaction
- The AI tool used
- The prompt or a representative copy of the prompt
- A brief description of what the AI tool is being used for
- Whether or not it was used, modified, or rejected

Interactions used that have no meaningful or significant impact on 
    the project do not need to be Logged

- Where the Log Lives -
The prompt log will be stored in "ai-prompt-log" or 
    "/workspaces/engineering-playbook/ai-prompt-log"

- Responsibilty - 
The team member who used the AI tool is responsible for recording the
    interaction. The person responsible for the related issue or pull
    request is responsible for ensuring that significant AI use has been 
    logged before the work in considered complete for the session

Team members should update the prompt log as part of their normal 
    development workflow rather than waiting until the end of the 
    project

3. AI-Assisted Code Review Policy
AI-assisted code review does not satisfy the team's required rule of 
    "one team-member must review and approve the pull-request"

AI code review can provide useful additional feedback, but it can also
    miss project-specific requirements, misunderstand the intended 
    behavior, or identify issues that are not actually relevant to the project.
    Therefore, an AI review is considered a supplemental review, while the
    team-member's approval remains the formal requirement for merging a 
    pull request.

For routine changes, an AI review may speed up the process, but at least one
    team-member must approve the pull request before it is merged.